@page "/workflow-run"

<CatHead Title="Watching a run">
    A run followed as it happens: an overview of its stages for the people who will never open a step,
    every step under it, the conversation the run started with, and why each decision went the way it did.
    The automation sends an event each time something changes; the app keeps the latest state of each
    step and binds it to <code>data-state</code>. Everything on screen is the
    <a href="/workflow">workflow</a> markup and the rest of the library &mdash; nothing here is drawn by
    a script.
</CatHead>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Interop.WorkflowLiveRun" Title="A support call, from the phone to the engineer on call" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        Press play. A caller reports that no card terminal in their store works; the voice agent asks,
        listens, looks it up and tries a fix, turn by turn, then opens a ticket and reads the number back.
        Three checks triage the ticket side by side, the agent weighs where it goes and pages the engineer
        on call, and the run waits for them to acknowledge before it hands over. Step through it one event at
        a time to tell the story at your own pace.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Two diagrams of one run.</strong> The overview is five steps, <code>.workflow--fill</code> so
        it fits; the detail is <code>.workflow--sm</code> with <code>data-follow</code>, so it scrolls to
        the step at work as the run moves. Both are the same markup with a different state dictionary
        behind them, and a stage's state is simply set alongside its steps'.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>The component holds only what arrived.</strong> A dictionary of step to state and one of
        step to its meta line; <code>S("assess")</code> is bound to that step's <code>data-state</code>,
        and a step nobody has mentioned renders no attribute at all, which is the state for a step not
        reached. The script here plays the events back on a timer; an app receives them.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>How much moves is the reader&rsquo;s choice.</strong> The controls above the card set
        <code>.workflow--flow-off</code> on both diagrams, and
        <code>--workflow-flow-speed</code> and <code>--workflow-flow-packets</code> in their
        <code>style</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Nothing here is a component.</strong> The card, the status, both diagrams, the transcript, the
        decision and the log are the library&rsquo;s classes on the app&rsquo;s own markup, so a different
        screen is a different arrangement of the same parts &mdash; the wall screen below is one.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.WorkflowRun.WallScreen" Title="On a wall screen or a tablet" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        The same run, shown rather than read: <code>.workflow--xl</code> for the back of the room,
        <code>.workflow--fill</code> so the stages fit the screen, and <code>.workflow--focus</code> so only the
        step at work opens &mdash; the others keep their heads and the detail every node carries stays out of
        the way until its step is the one happening. The status above it is an
        <a href="/agent-status"><code>.agent-status--xl</code></a>. For a screen bigger still, scale the frame
        with <code>zoom</code> rather than restating sizes: every wire, bracket and mark is arithmetic on the
        same numbers, so it all grows together.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Full screen is a button.</strong> <code>data-fullscreen</code> on a button puts the element
        its <code>aria-controls</code> names into full screen, or the whole page without one, and keeps its
        <code>aria-pressed</code> right however the reader leaves. Make that element a surface &mdash; here a
        <code>.card</code> &mdash; because full screen shows it alone on a black backdrop. On a kiosk or a TV
        that boots into the browser, the page is already the whole screen and needs no button at all.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatSnippet Name="Interop/WorkflowEvents.txt" Title="Receiving the events">
    <p>
        The automation tells the app what it is doing with an HTTP step after each node &mdash; the step&rsquo;s
        id, its new state, and a line for the meta. The endpoint checks the automation&rsquo;s key and hands
        the event to a singleton; every component showing that run applies it on its own circuit. Nothing
        about the diagram crosses the wire but those three fields.
    </p>
</CatSnippet>
